<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ukrainisation</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1923">1923</a>–<a href="/years/1933">1933</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Ukrainization</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ukrainisation was the Soviet policy of the 1920s and early 1930s that promoted the Ukrainian language and ethnic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukrainians">Ukrainians</a> in the schools, press, culture and administration of the Ukrainian SSR. It was the Ukrainian form of korenizatsiia, the Bolshevik policy of rooting Soviet power among the non-Russian nationalities. Stalin reversed it from 1932–1933, and many of its leaders were purged; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mykola-skrypnyk">Mykola Skrypnyk</a> took his own life in 1933.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ukrainisation.jpg" alt="Ukrainisation" width="320" height="265" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johann Homann · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ukrania_quae_et_Terra_Cosaccorum_cum_vicinis_Walachiae,_Moldoviae,_Johann_Baptiste_Homann_(Nuremberg,_1720).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The term covers policies and practices that increase the use of the Ukrainian language and promote Ukrainian culture in education, publishing, government and religion. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, laws eliminated Ukrainian from state institutions and schools. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution">Russian Revolution of 1917</a> a Ukrainian state emerged but could do little culturally while fighting for survival. Under the Hetmanate of Pavlo Skoropadskyi in 1918, in spite of its relative instability, millions of Ukrainian textbooks were printed, and many Ukrainian schools, two universities and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-academy-of-sciences-of-ukraine">Ukrainian Academy of Sciences</a> were founded.</p><p>The Bolshevik authorities were at first sceptical of the revival of non-Russian nations. Noticing that the indigenous peoples of the former empire viewed becoming part of a new Russian state negatively, the Soviet government began a policy of indigenisation to extend the Communist Party network on non-Russian lands with the involvement of the local population. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> this brought a short period of Ukrainisation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Ukrainisation reached the armed forces, though with less success. A School of Red Commanders was organised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharkiv">Kharkiv</a> to advance Ukrainian officers, the military district's newspaper Chervona Armiya appeared in Ukrainian until the mid-1930s, and even the Soviet Pacific Fleet had a Ukrainian department.</p><p>In the early 1930s the policy was gradually revised. Efforts by many Soviet Ukrainian politicians to extend it into the Russian republic had fuelled unrest among Russian workers, and from the 1930s it was reduced in tempo and carried out quietly to avoid Russian resentment. Sporadic purges of Ukrainian government, educational and cultural institutions accompanied the shift, and tens of thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukrainians">Ukrainians</a> were arrested or expelled from the party in the national terror of 1933. In the mid-1960s moderate efforts returned in government, and Ukrainian use revived in education, publishing and culture.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>On 28 October 1989 the Supreme Soviet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> adopted a law making Ukrainian the only official language while guaranteeing protection to the other languages spoken in the republic. After independence in 1991 the law stayed in force, and the constitution of 1996 confirmed Ukrainian as the state language and guaranteed the free development and use of Russian and other minority languages.</p><p>Schooling in Ukrainian grew from 47.9 per cent of secondary pupils in 1990 to 1991 to 75.1 per cent by 2003 to 2004, and higher education in Ukrainian from 7 per cent of students in 1990 to 1991 to 80.1 per cent at university level by 2003 to 2004. Russian remained a required course in secondary schools. Language was an issue in the presidential elections of 1994 and 2004, and later laws on education and on the state language in 2017 and 2019 were followed by a law passed on 8 December 2022 amending the language rights of national minorities.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ukrainization” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The 1920s brought <b>Ukrainisation</b> in schools and culture, but Stalin's forced collectivisation caused the famine of 1932 and 1933, the Holodomor, which killed millions.</blockquote><a href="/state/ukrainian-ssr">Ukrainian SSR</a></li></ul></section>
